{{also|Johnny}}
==English==

===Etymology===
{{alternative case form of|en|Johnny|nodot=a}}, diminutive of {{m|en|John}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|GenAm}} {{IPA|en|/ˈd͡ʒɑni/}}
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˈd͡ʒɒni/}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɒni}}
* {{hyph|en|john|ny}}
* {{audio|en|En-au-johnny.ogg|Audio (AU)}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|UK|slang}} A [[condom]].
# {{lb|en|New Zealand|pejorative}} An inexperienced new worker, usually an immigrant.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1913|author=Katherine Mansfield|title=Millie
|passage=Willie Cox said they found him in the barn, shot bang through the head, and the young English '''johnny''' who'd been on the station learning farming - disappeared.}}
# A [[hospital gown]]: a gown with a back opening closed with snaps or ties, worn by hospital patients.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1991|author=Stephen King|title=Needful Things
|passage=His hand shook. A little water spilled down the front of the hospital '''johnny''' Sean wore.}}
# {{lb|en|US|slang|dated}} {{synonym of|en|john|nodot=a}}: a [[toilet]], [[lavatory]], [[outhouse]], or [[chamber pot]].
#* '''1935''', {{w|John O'Hara}}, ''{{w|BUtterfield 8}}'',<!--sic--> Ch. ix, page 279:
#*: The women's toilet (as distinguished from the [[ladies' room]] in a [[speakeasy]], the [[johnny]] at school, the [[little girls' room]] at a party in an apartment, and the [[wash-my-hands]] on a train) was clean enough.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1935|author=w:John O'Hara|title=w:Appointment in Samarra|chapter=iv|page=98
|passage=Kitty Hofman came in the '''johnny'''.}}
# {{lb|en|Ireland|slang|archaic}} A half-glass of [[whisky]].
#* '''1867''', William Dool Killen, ''Memoir of John Edgar'' (page 108)
#*: [H]e declared, like a real Irishman, that 'if he were a living man when he died, he would not give a single '''johnny''' of whisky either at his wake or his funeral.'

